WEBVTT

NOTE
Captions for "Threads of Infinity — Leonhard Euler" (2 min 28 s).
The film is built from ~10-second micro-beats over a music bed, so exact sync was
estimated from the script rather than the audio. Timings are approximate and easy to
nudge — adjust any cue as needed. Speaker names are shown for accessibility.

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:09.000
BERNOULLI: Euler was my friend — and the bravest question-asker I knew. Watch what a single question can pull.

2
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:24.000
BERNOULLI: In Basel I told Euler the best tool in mathematics: a good question.

3
00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:28.200
EULER: And the courage to follow it.

4
00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:33.500
BERNOULLI: Every curve has its own voice, Leonhard. Listen, and it will tell you how it changes.

5
00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:38.000
EULER: It rises, then falls … the slope sings where it bends. That's the sound of change.

6
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:47.200
BERNOULLI: A puzzle arrived from Königsberg — seven bridges no one could cross without repeating. Euler didn't just try; he reimagined the problem.

7
00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:52.500
EULER: If I mark each island as a point and each bridge as a line between them, the question becomes simple —

8
00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:57.000
EULER: it's not about walking paths, but connections. Some places have too many lines; a loop is impossible.

9
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:06.200
BERNOULLI: Numbers that go on forever … how could their sum reach anything finite? Euler saw a bridge between infinity and geometry.

10
00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:15.500
EULER: When I joined the infinite with the wave of sine, they met in perfect harmony — and from that song came pi.

11
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:24.800
BERNOULLI: Euler found a count that never changes: corners, edges, faces — different shapes, same truth.

12
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:33.800
EULER: I turned the shapes, stretched them, squashed them — but their difference was always two. That number holds them all together.

13
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:39.000
BERNOULLI: Kings and Queens sought his mind. In Berlin, Frederick demanded precision;

14
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:43.800
BERNOULLI: in St. Petersburg, Catherine welcomed imagination. Euler gave them both.

15
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:47.800
KING: Mathematics is fine, Euler.

16
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:51.000
CATHERINE THE GREAT: But it must serve reason and rule.

17
00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:55.500
EULER: Or imagination to lift hearts as well as armies.

18
00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:59.500
SCRIBE: Another theorem, Master?

19
00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:04.500
EULER: My eyes fade, but numbers shine within my mind.

20
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:11.000
BERNOULLI: Now the thread is yours. Ask a brave question — and pull.
